Electricity comprises the physical effects linked to the existence and movement of matter bearing electric charge. It is intertwined with magnetism, both forming the broader phenomenon of electromagnetism as outlined by Maxwell’s equations. Numerous everyday occurrences—such as lightning, static discharge, electric heating, and various other electric discharges—stem from electrical principles. A charge, whether positive or negative, creates an electric field, and the flow of these charges constitutes an electric current that generates a magnetic field. In most practical cases, a force acts on a charge according to Coulomb’s law, and electric potential is measured in volts. Modern technology relies heavily on electricity, primarily for:  
